--- a/docs/requirements.rst
+++ b/docs/requirements.rst
@@ -61,6 +61,28 @@ Usage
     >>> requirements1 == requirements2
     True
 
+Use ``__replace__`` to create a requirement with selected fields changed:
+
+.. doctest::
+
+    >>> req = Requirement('requests[security]>=2.0; python_version >= "3"')
+    >>> req.__replace__(name="flask")
+    <Requirement('flask[security]>=2.0; python_version >= "3"')>
+    >>> from packaging.specifiers import SpecifierSet
+    >>> req.__replace__(specifier=SpecifierSet(">=3.0"), marker=None)
+    <Requirement('requests[security]>=3.0')>
+
+On Python 3.13 and later, ``copy.replace(req, name="flask")`` also works.
+The supported keyword arguments are ``name``, ``url``, ``extras``, ``specifier``,
+and ``marker``, using the same types as the corresponding attributes.
+Unspecified fields are kept, and the original requirement is unchanged.
+The new fields must form a valid requirement and round-trip through parsing
+without changing other fields; otherwise :class:`InvalidRequirement` is raised.
+The result is normalized as when parsing a requirement string. Mutable fields
+are independent copies, and explicit prerelease settings on the specifier set
+and its individual specifiers are preserved. Subclasses are reconstructed using
+their constructor.
+
 .. versionchanged:: 23.2
 
     When a requirement is specified with a URL, the :class:`Requirement` class
